        "type": "text",
        "text": "# Form Behavior & Patterns\n\nTopic: forms\nAliases: inputs, validation, form errors, textarea, submission, multi-step forms, step by step, progress indicator\n\n# Form Behavior & Patterns\n\n## Place Forms After Intent\n\nOn browse, list, overview, and manage pages, a creation or configuration form is a secondary task. Keep it behind a clearly labeled CTA and reveal it in a popover, dialog, sheet, drawer, or dedicated route after the user asks for it. Do not let a dormant form take permanent vertical space or push the content the user came to manage below the fold.\n\nChoose the disclosure surface by task size and context:\n\n- Use a popover for a tiny form anchored to its trigger.\n- Use a dialog or sheet for a focused sub-task that should return to the current page.\n- Use a dedicated page for a multi-step task or genuinely new context.\n\nInline forms are correct when form completion is the page's P0 task: sign-in, checkout, onboarding, settings, a focused editor, or search and filters that operate the current view.\n\n```tsx\n// ❌ Bad: the browse page always pays the space cost\n<>\n  <CreateFlowForm />\n  <FlowList />\n</>\n\n// ✅ Good: creation appears only after explicit intent\n<>\n  <Button onClick={() => setOpen(true)}>New flow</Button>\n  <FlowList />\n  <Dialog open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen}>\n    <CreateFlowForm />\n  </Dialog>\n</>\n```\n\n## Form Submission\n\n### Enter Key Submits\n- When a text input is focused, Enter submits the form\n- If it's the only input: Enter submits\n- If there are multiple inputs: Enter on the last input submits\n- Wrap inputs with `<form>` element to enable this behavior\n\n```html\n<form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\n  <input type=\"text\" />\n  <button type=\"submit\">Submit</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n### Textarea Behavior\n- In `<textarea>`, Enter inserts a new line\n- ⌘+Enter (Mac) or Ctrl+Enter (Windows) submits the form\n- Implement with keyboard event handler\n\n```tsx\n<textarea\n  onKeyDown={(e) => {\n    if (e.key === 'Enter' && (e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey)) {\n      handleSubmit();\n    }\n  }}\n/>\n```\n\n### Let Textareas Grow With Content\n- Consider letting textareas grow with the content instead of introducing nested scrolling. It often creates a smoother writing experience and keeps forms easier to scan.\n- `field-sizing: content` makes a textarea auto-size to its text, so the whole entry stays visible without an inner scrollbar.\n- `field-sizing` is not yet baseline (Firefox ignores it), so gate the auto-grow rule behind `@supports` and give non-supporting browsers a `resize: vertical` handle as a graceful fallback.\n\n```css\ntextarea {\n  /* Fallback for browsers without field-sizing (e.g. Firefox). */\n  resize: vertical;\n}\n\n@supports (field-sizing: content) {\n  textarea {\n    field-sizing: content;\n    max-height: 20lh; /* bound runaway growth, then scroll */\n    overflow-y: auto;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Submission Rule\n- Keep submit button **enabled** until submission starts\n- Once submission starts:\n  - Disable button\n  - Show spinner/loading indicator\n  - Keep original button label visible\n  - Include idempotency key in request\n- Re-enable after response (success or error)\n\n```tsx\n<button type=\"submit\" disabled={isSubmitting}>\n  {isSubmitting && <Spinner />}\n  Submit\n</button>\n```\n\n### Don't Pre-Disable Submit\n- Don't disable submit button for incomplete forms\n- Let users submit incomplete forms to surface validation feedback\n- Show validation errors on submit attempt\n\n## Labels & Inputs\n\n### Every Input Has a Label\n- Every control must have a `<label>` or accessible label\n- Use `<label>` element associated with `for` attribute\n- Or use `aria-label` / `aria-labelledby`\n\n```html\n<label for=\"email\">Email</label>\n<input id=\"email\" type=\"email\" />\n```\n\n### Label Activation\n- Clicking a `<label>` should focus the associated control\n- Automatically works with proper `for`/`id` pairing\n- Increases hit target size\n\n### No Dead Zones on Controls\n- Checkboxes, radios: avoid dead zones between label and control\n- Make entire label + control a single clickable area\n- Use `<label>` wrapper or increase padding\n\n```html\n<label class=\"checkbox-wrapper\">\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" />\n  <span>Label text</span>\n</label>\n```\n\n```css\n.checkbox-wrapper {\n  display: flex;\n  align-items: center;\n  gap: 8px;\n  padding: 8px;\n  cursor: pointer;\n}\n```\n\n## Input Behavior\n\n### Don't Block Typing\n- Even if field only accepts numbers, allow any input\n- Show validation feedback instead of blocking keystrokes\n- Blocking keystrokes is confusing (user gets no explanation)\n\n```tsx\n// ❌ Bad: Blocks input\n<input onKeyPress={(e) => {\n  if (!/[0-9]/.test(e.key)) e.preventDefault();\n}} />\n\n// ✅ Good: Allow input, show validation\n<input\n  type=\"text\"\n  value={value}\n  onChange={(e) => setValue(e.target.value)}\n  aria-invalid={!/^\\d+$/.test(value)}\n/>\n{!/^\\d+$/.test(value) && <span>Must be a number</span>}\n```\n\n### Trim Input Values\n- Text input methods can add trailing whitespace\n- Trim values before validation to avoid confusing errors\n- Especially important for emails, usernames, codes\n\n```tsx\nconst handleSubmit = () => {\n  const email = emailInput.value.trim();\n  // Validate trimmed value\n};\n```\n\n### Allow Paste Everywhere\n- Never disable paste in inputs\n- Users need to paste passwords, 2FA codes, etc.\n- Validate after paste, don't block it\n\n## Input Types & Attributes\n\n### Use Correct Input Types\n- Set appropriate `type` attribute:\n  - `type=\"email\"` for emails\n  - `type=\"password\"` for passwords\n  - `type=\"tel\"` for phone numbers\n  - `type=\"number\"` for numeric input\n  - `type=\"url\"` for URLs\n  - `type=\"search\"` for search boxes\n- Enables better mobile keyboards and validation\n\n### Use inputmode for Keyboards\n- Set `inputmode` for better mobile keyboard\n- Works with `type=\"text\"` when you need custom validation\n\n```html\n<input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"numeric\" /> <!-- Number keyboard -->\n<input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" /> <!-- Decimal keyboard -->\n<input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"email\" />   <!-- Email keyboard -->\n<input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"tel\" />     <!-- Phone keyboard -->\n```\n\n### Autocomplete Attributes\n- Set `autocomplete` to enable browser autofill\n- Helps users fill forms faster\n- Important for accessibility\n\n```html\n<input type=\"email\" autocomplete=\"email\" />\n<input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"given-name\" />\n<input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"family-name\" />\n<input type=\"tel\" autocomplete=\"tel\" />\n<input type=\"password\" autocomplete=\"current-password\" />\n<input type=\"password\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" />\n```\n\n### Spellcheck Selectively\n- Disable spellcheck for:\n  - Emails\n  - Usernames\n  - Codes/tokens\n  - Technical input\n- Enable for long-form text content\n\n```html\n<input type=\"email\" spellcheck=\"false\" />\n<textarea spellcheck=\"true\"></textarea>\n```\n\n### Prevent Password Manager Interference\n- For non-auth fields (like search), avoid reserved names\n- Use `autocomplete=\"off\"` or specific tokens\n- Don't use `name=\"password\"` for non-password fields\n\n```html\n<!-- Search input that shouldn't trigger password manager -->\n<input\n  type=\"text\"\n  name=\"query\"\n  autocomplete=\"off\"\n/>\n\n<!-- OTP field -->\n<input\n  type=\"text\"\n  autocomplete=\"one-time-code\"\n/>\n```\n\n## Validation & Errors\n\n### Error Placement\n- Show errors **next to their fields**, not just at top of form\n- On submit, **focus the first error**\n- Use `aria-invalid` and `aria-describedby`\n\n```html\n<input\n  id=\"email\"\n  type=\"email\"\n  aria-invalid={hasError}\n  aria-describedby=\"email-error\"\n/>\n{hasError && (\n  <span id=\"email-error\" role=\"alert\">\n    Please enter a valid email\n  </span>\n)}\n```\n\n### HTML Form Validation\n- Use `required` attribute when appropriate\n- Leverage browser validation before custom validation\n- Provides consistent UX across browsers\n\n```html\n<input type=\"email\" required />\n<input type=\"text\" pattern=\"[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}\" required />\n```\n\n## Placeholder Patterns\n\n### Placeholders Show Example Values\n- End with ellipsis to signal emptiness\n- Show pattern or example: `+1 (123) 456-7890`\n- Or: `sk-012345679…`\n- Don't use as replacement for labels\n\n```html\n<label for=\"phone\">Phone</label>\n<input\n  id=\"phone\"\n  type=\"tel\"\n  placeholder=\"+1 (123) 456-7890\"\n/>\n```\n\n## Password Fields & 2FA\n\n### Password Manager Compatibility\n- Ensure password fields work with managers\n- Use correct `autocomplete` values\n- Allow pasting one-time codes\n\n```html\n<!-- Login -->\n<input type=\"email\" autocomplete=\"email\" />\n<input type=\"password\" autocomplete=\"current-password\" />\n\n<!-- Registration -->\n<input type=\"email\" autocomplete=\"email\" />\n<input type=\"password\" autocomplete=\"new-password\" />\n\n<!-- 2FA code -->\n<input type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"one-time-code\" />\n```\n\n## Unsaved Changes Warning\n\n### Warn Before Navigation\n- When form has unsaved changes, warn before:\n  - Navigation to another page\n  - Browser tab close\n  - Browser refresh\n- Use `beforeunload` event\n\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const handler = (e: BeforeUnloadEvent) => {\n    if (hasUnsavedChanges) {\n      e.preventDefault();\n      e.returnValue = '';\n    }\n  };\n  window.addEventListener('beforeunload', handler);\n  return () => window.removeEventListener('beforeunload', handler);\n}, [hasUnsavedChanges]);\n```\n\n## Windows-Specific Issues\n\n### Windows `<select>` Background\n- Windows has dark mode contrast bugs with native `<select>`\n- Explicitly set `background-color` and `color`\n\n```css\nselect {\n  background-color: var(--bg);\n  color: var(--text);\n}\n```\n\n## Form Organization\n\n### Inline Editing\n- Prefer inline editing over separate edit forms\n- Toggle between view/edit mode in place\n- Reduces context switching\n\n### Progressive Disclosure\n- Show additional fields only when needed\n- Don't overwhelm users with all options upfront\n- Reveal complexity gradually\n\n## Multi-Step Forms\n\nWhether the flow should be stepped at all is a pattern decision: see `interaction-patterns.md`\n(Wizard & Stepper). This section is the mechanics once you have decided to split it.\n\n### Split the Work Into Steps\n- Break a long form into steps, and keep one decision on each step\n- A short step reads as less work than a long page, even when the field count is the same\n- Persist every answer between steps, and let the user go back without losing it\n- Do not add a step to make the flow look short. The user counts the questions, not the screens\n\n### Keyboard Runs the Whole Flow\n- Wrap each step in its own `<form>`, so the submit action of the step advances to the next step\n- Enter submits from a text field. Inside a `<textarea>` it stays a newline, and Cmd or Ctrl with Enter submits\n- Move focus to the new step, and give each step a heading a screen reader announces\n- Arrow keys move through a radio group, and each move also changes the selection\n- A selection an arrow key made must not advance the step\n- Enter on a radio is not a reliable submit across browsers. Give the group its own Enter handler, or let Tab reach the Continue button\n- The Continue button obeys the submission rule above. It locks when the step submits, so a second Enter cannot skip a step\n- Every step works with Tab alone. Escape closes a helper panel, and it never discards the answers\n\n```tsx\nfunction Step({ stepId, title, isSubmitting, onNext, children }: StepProps) {\n  const heading = useRef<HTMLHeadingElement>(null);\n\n  // A step is a new screen, so focus moves to its heading on every step.\n  // The step id is the dependency: a parent that reuses this instance and\n  // swaps the title would otherwise leave focus on the step before it.\n  useEffect(() => heading.current?.focus(), [stepId]);\n\n  return (\n    <form onSubmit={(event) => { event.preventDefault(); onNext(); }}>\n      <h1 ref={heading} tabIndex={-1}>{title}</h1>\n      {children}\n      {/* Locked while the step submits, so a second Enter cannot skip a step.\n          The label stays, and the spinner says the save is still running. */}\n      <button type=\"submit\" disabled={isSubmitting}>\n        {isSubmitting && <Spinner />}\n        Continue\n      </button>\n    </form>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n### Show Progress\n- Say where the user is and how much is left: `Step 2 of 4`, a stepper, or a progress bar\n- Mark the finished steps, and let the user open one again to change the answer\n- Use `<progress>`, or `role=\"progressbar\"` with `aria-valuenow`, `aria-valuemin`, and `aria-valuemax`\n- Keep the total honest. A bar that fills to 90 percent and then adds three steps costs you trust\n\n```html\n<p id=\"step-count\">Step 2 of 4</p>\n<progress value=\"2\" max=\"4\" aria-labelledby=\"step-count\"></progress>\n```\n\n### Let the User Skip\n- Make a step skippable unless its answer blocks the next step\n- Put the skip control next to the primary button, in plain words: `Skip for now`\n- Ask again later, in the place where the answer does the work\n- Prefill what you already know, such as the account email, and let the user confirm instead of type\n- Test each required step: ask what breaks if the answer arrives later. If nothing breaks, make it optional\n\n### Make It Feel Fast\n- Advance as soon as a pointer picks the answer, on a step with one choice. The extra Next click is pure cost\n- Keep the Continue button for the keyboard, because arrow keys change the selection while the user still reads the options\n- Save in the background. Do not hold the next step behind the network call for the last one\n- Hold the answer locally, flush a pending save when the step unmounts, and keep the writes in order. Show a retry when one fails\n- Wait for the pending writes before the flow finishes, so completion cannot race the last answer\n- Prefetch the data of the next step while the user reads the current one\n- Keep the step transition short, about 150ms to 200ms, with ease-out. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` change the step at once. See `ui/perceived-speed.md`\n- Do not put a full-page spinner between two steps. Keep the frame, and change only the step content\n\n## Controlled vs Uncontrolled\n\n### Prefer Uncontrolled Inputs\n- Uncontrolled inputs are more performant\n- Less re-renders on every keystroke\n- Use `defaultValue` instead of `value`\n- Access value via ref when needed\n\n```tsx\n// Uncontrolled (preferred for performance)\nconst inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);\n\nconst handleSubmit = () => {\n  const value = inputRef.current?.value;\n};\n\n<input ref={inputRef} defaultValue=\"\" />\n```\n\n```tsx\n// Controlled (use when you need real-time validation)\nconst [value, setValue] = useState('');\n\n<input\n  value={value}\n  onChange={(e) => setValue(e.target.value)}\n/>\n```\n\n## Checklist\n\n### Structure\n- [ ] Inputs wrapped in `<form>` element\n- [ ] Enter key submits form\n- [ ] Textarea: ⌘/Ctrl+Enter submits\n- [ ] Every input has a `<label>`\n- [ ] Clicking label focuses input\n\n### Submission\n- [ ] Submit button enabled until submission starts\n- [ ] Disabled + spinner during submission\n- [ ] Don't pre-disable for incomplete forms\n- [ ] Idempotency key included in request\n\n### Input Behavior\n- [ ] Don't block typing (validate instead)\n- [ ] Allow paste everywhere\n- [ ] Trim values before validation\n- [ ] No dead zones on checkboxes/radios\n\n### Attributes\n- [ ] Correct `type` attribute set\n- [ ] `inputmode` for mobile keyboards\n- [ ] `autocomplete` for autofill\n- [ ] `spellcheck` disabled for technical fields\n- [ ] `required` for required fields\n\n### Validation\n- [ ] Errors shown next to fields\n- [ ] Focus first error on submit\n- [ ] `aria-invalid` and `aria-describedby` set\n- [ ] HTML validation enabled\n\n### Special Cases\n- [ ] Password manager compatibility\n- [ ] 2FA code autofill supported\n- [ ] Unsaved changes warning\n- [ ] Windows `<select>` background set\n\n### Organization\n- [ ] Inline editing where appropriate\n- [ ] Progressive disclosure used\n- [ ] Prefer uncontrolled inputs for performance\n\n### Multi-Step Forms\n- [ ] Long forms broken into steps\n- [ ] Enter advances to the next step\n- [ ] Focus moves to each new step\n- [ ] Progress and remaining steps shown\n- [ ] Optional steps carry a visible skip control\n- [ ] Answers saved in the background, next step prefetched\n- [ ] A pending save flushed when the step unmounts, with a retry on failure\n\nYou have loaded design guidance in this session and not reviewed the result yet. When the screen exists, call `get-review-rules`, then `check-comprehension` and `inspect-spacing`. For a dashboard, deploy the exact source to a public HTTPS preview; `finalize-dashboard-review` must return VALID after Better Design captures it at 390, 1440, and 1728. Confirm every nav item, link, tab and button works. Fix every critical and serious finding before you present it."
